# M1: Codex findings whose quoted ranges are not co-located with any primary/replica record (never in a multi-run region).
# Each is normalized to the claim it attributes to the quoted text; all remain singletons (NO_CO_LOCATED_RECORD_FROM_OTHER_RUN).
CX X:BF-M1-0021 | The EF Core module states the property of one commit per use case (reported as possibly stronger than a commit boundary that allows justified multi-commit transactions).
CX X:BF-M1-0038 | The post-tool hook surfaces at most 25 diagnostics (reported risk of hiding later findings).
CX X:BF-M1-0039 | Shell-driven manifest-change checking compares against saved Git state (reported: no baseline outside Git).
CX X:BF-M1-0047 | The architecture check classifies project roles by naming and package markers (reported: custom-named projects become unknown).
CX X:BF-M1-0063 | The shared checker library skips a fixed set of directories (reported: manifests there are never inspected).
CX X:BF-M1-0064 | Active instruction-root resolution uses the nearest instruction root within the Git boundary (reported scope ambiguity for nested trees).
CX X:BF-M1-0065 | Project references are read by a custom XML reader without full MSBuild condition/import semantics (reported divergence from effective references).
CX X:BF-M1-0066 | Co-change state derives from uncommitted Git files and Git-backed state storage (reported: committed task changes drop out of stop-hook scope).
CX X:BF-M1-0068 | Dependency-command classification strips heredocs and parses shell lexically (reported: indirect installs are not recognized).
CX X:BF-M1-0081 | The error-handling example maps request cancellation to status 499 (reported as a status choice beyond the cancellation rule).
CX X:BF-M1-0088 | The JWT-validation example includes a minimum key length and an expiration bound (reported copy risk despite the numeric-example disclaimer).
CX X:BF-M1-0096 | The EF Core mapping example embeds a numeric precision and an index without a local product-approval caveat (reported copy risk).
CX X:BF-M1-0109 | The Serilog bootstrap sample logs a fatal startup exception without rethrowing or setting a failure exit status (reported failure-visibility uncertainty).
